      "latePostId": "69e73b9772ab25e722ea8116",
      "content": "You can buy US government bonds directly from the government — no bank, no broker, zero fees 💸\n\nTreasuryDirect.gov — what it is:\n\nWhat you can buy:\n→ Treasury Bills (T-bills): 4-52 week maturities\n→ Treasury Notes: 2-10 year maturities\n→ Treasury Bonds: 20-30 year maturities\n→ TIPS (inflation-protected)\n→ I-bonds (inflation-adjusted savings bonds)\n\nHow it compares to brokerages:\n→ Same securities, same yields (government-set rates)\n→ Brokerage: takes a spread or charges a fee\n→ TreasuryDirect: $0 fees, $0 markup\n→ Minimum purchase: $100\n\nI-bonds in 2022:\n→ Inflation peaked → I-bond composite rate: 9.62%\n→ No bank was offering anywhere near this\n→ $10,000 limit/year per Social Security number\n→ Additional $5,000 through federal tax refund\n→ Held for 1 year min; 3-month interest penalty if cashed before 5 years\n\nLimitations (important):\n→ Interface: famously clunky, looks like 2003-era government website\n→ No secondary market: must hold to maturity or cash out with penalty\n→ Transfers to brokerage: possible but cumbersome\n→ For traders who need liquidity: brokerage is better\n\nWho this is ideal for:\n→ Buy-and-hold investors\n→ Emergency fund earning better yield than HYSA\n→ Inflation protection (I-bonds)\n→ Avoiding broker relationship for simple bond ladder\n\nThe history:\n→ Available since 1986\n→ Most people have never heard of it despite being a federal program\n→ Total outstanding: over $1 trillion in retail savings bonds\n\n#moneyfacts #treasuryDirect #ibonds #personalfinance #investing #financialliteracy #savingmoney #treasurybonds #fixedincome #financialfacts",
