      "latePostId": "69e88ea9f85478fe3b9c4419",
      "content": "Most Americans misunderstand how tax brackets work — and some have declined raises because of it 💸\n\nMarginal vs Effective Tax Rate:\n\nThe myth:\n→ 'I got a raise into the 22% bracket — now I'll pay more in taxes'\n→ 'If I earn over $47K, everything gets taxed at 22%'\n→ 'I might take home less if I earn more'\n→ All of these are wrong\n\nHow marginal rates actually work (2024 single filer):\n→ First $11,600: taxed at 10%\n→ $11,601 to $47,150: taxed at 12%\n→ $47,151 to $100,525: taxed at 22%\n→ $100,526 to $191,950: taxed at 24%\n→ Higher brackets continue up to 37%\n\nA concrete example — $55,000 income:\n→ 10% on first $11,600 = $1,160\n→ 12% on next $35,550 ($11,600 to $47,150) = $4,266\n→ 22% on remaining $7,850 ($47,150 to $55,000) = $1,727\n→ Total federal tax: ~$7,153\n→ Effective rate: 13% (not 22%)\n\nThe key insight:\n→ Your marginal rate = rate on your LAST dollar\n→ Your effective rate = rate on your AVERAGE dollar\n→ They are never the same (unless you're in the 10% bracket)\n→ A raise ALWAYS increases take-home pay (assuming fixed credits)\n\nWhen a raise CAN reduce net pay:\n→ When it phases out specific tax credits\n→ Earned Income Credit: phases out as income rises\n→ Premium Tax Credit (ACA): phases out above certain thresholds\n→ But this is not the bracket — it's the credit phase-out\n→ The bracket itself never reduces take-home pay\n\nStandard deduction reminder (2024):\n→ Single: $14,600 deducted before any tax applies\n→ Married filing jointly: $29,200\n→ $100K income → ~$85,400 taxable income → ~18% effective rate\n\n#moneyfacts #taxes #taxbracket #marginalrate #financialliteracy #personalfinance #taxfacts #incometax #financialeducation #moneytips",
