      "latePostId": "69e73b9e6013b43447173746",
      "content": "You can collect Social Security even if you never worked a day — and millions of people don't know this 💸\n\nThe Spousal Social Security Benefit:\n\nHow it works:\n→ Based on your spouse's work record (not yours)\n→ Amount: up to 50% of spouse's Primary Insurance Amount (PIA) at their FRA\n→ Your spouse must have filed for their own benefits first\n→ Your own record: if higher than 50% of spouse's, Social Security pays you the higher amount\n→ You must be at least 62 to claim\n\nFull Retirement Age matters:\n→ Claim at 62: spousal benefit reduced (same early claiming penalty as own benefit)\n→ Claim at FRA: full 50% of spouse's PIA\n→ Waiting past FRA: NO additional credits (unlike your own benefit)\n→ Maximum spousal benefit = 50% of spouse's PIA. Period.\n\nSurvivor benefit (even more valuable):\n→ Spouse dies: you can claim 100% of their benefit\n→ Full survivor benefit available at FRA\n→ Strategy: delay your own benefit AND your spouse's benefit for maximum survivor protection\n\nThe divorced spouse rule:\n→ Married 10+ years?\n→ Divorced (not remarried)?\n→ You qualify for spousal/survivor benefits based on ex-spouse's record\n→ Completely independent — doesn't affect ex-spouse's benefits at all\n→ They don't even need to know you're claiming\n\nThe hidden rule:\n→ SSA will NOT tell you about the spousal benefit proactively\n→ You must know to ask\n→ Optimal strategy requires knowing both spouses' projected benefit amounts\n→ Claiming order matters — timing differences can mean $100,000+ lifetime income difference\n\nWho should know this:\n→ Stay-at-home parents/partners\n→ Low-earning spouses\n→ Anyone divorced after 10+ year marriage\n→ Widows and widowers\n\n#moneyfacts #socialSecurity #retirementplanning #spousalbenefit #financialliteracy #personalfinance #retirement #financialfacts #retirementfacts #divorcefinance",
