      "latePostId": "69e88ea4f85478fe3b9c4311",
      "content": "Your employer's 401k match might not be yours — yet 💸\n\nThe 401k Vesting Trap:\n\nHow vesting works:\n→ Your own contributions: always 100% yours, immediately\n→ Employer match: subject to vesting schedule\n→ Vesting = the percentage of employer contributions you actually own\n→ Before fully vested: some or all of the match can be forfeited\n\nTwo types of vesting schedules:\n\nCliff vesting:\n→ 0% owned until specific date (usually year 3)\n→ Then 100% immediately\n→ Leave day before cliff: forfeit 100% of employer match\n→ Leave day after cliff: keep 100% of employer match\n\nGraded vesting:\n→ Percentage increases each year\n→ Common schedule: 20% per year over 5 years\n→ Year 1: 20%, Year 2: 40%, Year 3: 60%, Year 4: 80%, Year 5: 100%\n→ Leave at year 2.5: keep 40% of all employer contributions received\n\nDOL minimum vesting requirements:\n→ Cliff: must be fully vested by year 3\n→ Graded: must be 20% at year 2, 100% by year 6\n→ Companies can be MORE generous, never less\n→ Immediate vesting (0-year cliff): a recruiting benefit some companies offer\n\nWhat you forfeit:\n→ Unvested portion returns to the plan as a 'forfeiture'\n→ Plan can use forfeitures to fund future employer contributions or pay plan expenses\n→ It disappears from your account permanently\n\nThe real dollar impact:\n→ 5% employer match on $80K salary = $4,000/year in match\n→ Leave after 2 years with 3-year cliff: forfeit 2 × $4,000 = $8,000\n→ Plus investment growth on that $8,000\n\nWhat to do before resigning:\n→ Ask HR: 'What is my current vested percentage?'\n→ Request your plan document (required to be provided on request)\n→ Calculate days until next vesting increment\n→ If you're 89% vested and 2 months from 100%: it may be worth waiting\n\n#moneyfacts #401k #vestingschedule #retirement #personalfinance #financialliteracy #jobchange #retirementplanning #employeebenefit #workplacefinance",
