      "latePostId": "69e88e9ef85478fe3b9c4223",
      "content": "You can sell a stock at a loss to save on taxes — but if you buy it back too soon, the IRS pretends the loss never happened 💸\n\nThe Wash Sale Rule (IRC Section 1091):\n\nThe basic rule:\n→ Sell security at a loss\n→ Buy same (or substantially identical) security within 30 days before OR after\n→ IRS disallows the loss deduction entirely\n→ Loss is not gone — it gets added to your cost basis\n→ But the timing benefit of harvesting it now is eliminated\n\nThe 30-day window goes BOTH directions:\n→ 30 days before the sale\n→ 30 days after the sale\n→ Total 61-day blackout period (including sale day)\n→ Most people only know about the 30 days after — the 30 days before catches many\n\nWhat counts as 'substantially identical':\n→ Same stock: obviously yes\n→ Vanguard S&P 500 fund sold → Fidelity S&P 500 fund bought: YES (same index)\n→ S&P 500 fund sold → Total Market fund bought: probably not (different index)\n→ S&P 500 fund sold → mid-cap fund bought: no\n→ Options on the same stock: yes\n\nHow most people trigger it accidentally:\n→ Sell stock at a loss → immediately rebuy (don't want to miss recovery)\n→ Automated dividend reinvestment buys back while selling at a loss\n→ Sell in taxable account → buy in IRA within 30 days\n→ (Yes: wash sale applies ACROSS accounts)\n\nThe crypto exception (currently):\n→ Crypto classified as property, not securities\n→ Wash sale rule does NOT apply\n→ Can sell Bitcoin at a loss, rebuy immediately, claim the tax loss\n→ Congress has proposed closing this loophole multiple times\n→ As of 2024: still open — check current law before acting\n\nThe correct workaround:\n→ Sell the fund\n→ Buy a similar but NOT substantially identical fund for 31 days\n→ Then sell that and rebuy original\n→ The 31-day alternative position preserves market exposure without wash sale\n\n#moneyfacts #taxlossharvesting #washsalerule #investing #taxfacts #personalfinance #financialliteracy #taxstrategy #capitalgains #investingtips",
