      "latePostId": "69e73b8f27bbcf51e3cff2ea",
      "content": "Paying rent on time doesn't build your credit score — and most people don't find out until they try to buy a house 💸\n\nWhy rent is invisible to credit bureaus:\n\nWhat DOES report automatically:\n→ Mortgage payments\n→ Car loans\n→ Credit card payments\n→ Student loans (federal and most private)\n→ Personal loans from banks/credit unions\n\nWhat does NOT report by default:\n→ Rent — no legal obligation for landlords to report\n→ Utilities (most)\n→ Cell phone bills (usually)\n→ Insurance premiums\n\nThe scale of the problem:\n→ Average rent: $1,500-$3,000/month in major cities\n→ Many renters pay this perfectly for 5-10 years\n→ Zero credit history generated\n→ Then they apply for a mortgage: thin credit file → higher rate or denial\n\nServices that report rent:\n→ Experian Boost: free, adds rent history to Experian file only\n→ RentReporters, Rental Kharma, LevelCredit: paid services (~$5-10/month)\n→ Some property management platforms (Rentler, Zego) report automatically — ask your landlord\n→ Some credit card companies now offer rent reporting as a perk\n\nCritical detail:\n→ Different bureaus don't share this data\n→ Reporting to Equifax doesn't affect TransUnion\n→ If your landlord uses a service: check which bureau(s) it reports to\n\nThe irony:\n→ First-generation renters most likely to lack credit history\n→ Also most likely to rent for longer before buying\n→ System structurally disadvantages those with least wealth\n\n#moneyfacts #creditScore #rent #financialliteracy #personalfinance #creditreport #rentingvsbuying #financialfacts #creditbuilding #moneylessons",
